{"kind":"assert","text":"Real call 2 result, for the note at 970c701c and the decision cccd205a. Observed run on Cloudflare Artifacts, ~/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-01-artifacts-token-call-2, SHA256SUMS b56d230d.\n\nEach case cloned one 30 MB repository of random bytes, throttled to about 150 KB/s by a local proxy on my side. Each clone took about 209 s.\n\n| Case | Clone | Head matches |\n|---|---|---|\n| Token for 3600 s, control | exit 0 | yes |\n| Token for 60 s, expires during the clone | exit 0 | yes |\n| Token for 3600 s, revoked by ID at 31 s | exit 0 | yes |\n\nIn the third case, 4.6 MB had reached the proxy when I revoked, and `git ls-remote` with that token then exited 128 with \"Invalid or expired token\".\n\nSo on Artifacts a clone in flight outlasts its token's expiry and its revocation. That has two results for the note:\n- The one-hour limit does not cut a slow clone that has started, in this run. The note's cost \"a 1 GB clone below 2.2 Mbit/s fails every time\" did not appear at this size.\n- A revocation stops new requests and does not stop a download under way. The note's sentence for Mara, \"Commits after that time are safe from these tokens\", holds only for commits the download had not reached. State that.\n\nLimits: one run of each case at one size. A buffer between the host and my proxy could hold some of the reply, and I did not measure one. A longer gap between token end and last byte did not run. The first attempt stopped after its control on one 401 from the token route, and the delete 3 s later worked, so I reran the other two cases on a new repository. Both repositories are deleted.\n\nDecision: real call 3 is not needed for the one-hour pin. GitHub already has about one hour, and the service has no ID to revoke by there, so its answer changes no choice in this design. It stays named as not run. The one-hour pin is now clear to join the next cohort. Do not start it yet. Add these results to the note at its next edit.","body":{"no_git